    "presentation": {
      "explanation": "Days of the week and months in German are **always capitalized** (as are ALL nouns in the German language!) and governed by the **AN / UM / IM Preposition Rule**.\n\nDays of the Week (All Masculine: *der* Montag, *der* Dienstag...):\n- **Montag** [MON-tahk / ˈmoːn.taːk] (Monday — Moon's day)\n- **Dienstag** [DEENS-tahk] (Tuesday — Day of Tiw/Tyr, Germanic war god)\n- **Mittwoch** [MIT-vokh] (Wednesday — literally 'mid-week'!)\n- **Donnerstag** [DON-ers-tahk] (Thursday — Donar's/Thor's day, god of thunder)\n- **Freitag** [FRY-tahk] (Friday — Freyja's day)\n- **Samstag** [ZAHMS-tahk] (Saturday — Sabbath / in North/East Germany also *Sonnabend*)\n- **Sonntag** [ZON-tahk] (Sunday — Sun's day)\n- **das Wochenende** (the weekend) / **unter der Woche** (on weekdays)\n\nMonths of the Year (All Masculine: *der* Januar, *der* Februar...):\nJanuar, Februar, März, April, Mai, Juni, Juli, August, September, Oktober, November, Dezember.\n\n**The AN / UM / IM Preposition Matrix:**\n1. **AM** (*an + dem*) — For Days, Parts of the Day, and Specific Dates:\n   * *am Montag* (on Monday), *am Abend* (in the evening), *am 15. Mai* (on May 15th), *am Wochenende* (on the weekend)\n   * Exception: *in der Nacht* (at night — feminine noun!)\n2. **UM** — For Clock Times:\n   * *um 8 Uhr* (at 8:00), *um halb neun* (at 8:30)\n3. **IM** (*in + dem*) — For Months and Seasons:\n   * *im Juli* (in July), *im Sommer* (in summer), *im Winter* (in winter).",
      "examples": [
        "Am Montag und am Mittwoch haben wir Deutschkurs um zehn Uhr. (On Monday and Wednesday we have German class at 10:00).",
        "Mein Geburtstag ist im August, am dreiundzwanzigsten August. (Notice: 'im August' [month], but 'am 23. August' [exact date]!).",
        "Was machst du am Wochenende? (What are you doing on the weekend?).",
        "Mittwoch is literally 'mid-week' (Mitte + Woche)."
      ],
      "mnemonics": [
        "Preposition Rule: AM Tage (days/dates), UM Uhrzeit (clock time), IM Monate/Jahreszeiten (months/seasons).",
        "Mittwoch = Mitte der Woche (Mid-week — the only day not ending in '-tag'!)."
      ],
      "culturalNotes": [
        "October 3rd (*3. Oktober — Tag der Deutschen Einheit*) is German Unity Day, celebrating the reunification of Germany in 1990.",
        "In Germany, all stores (except in train stations and airports) are legally closed on Sunday (*Sonntagsruhe*), dedicated to rest, outdoor walks, and family time."
      ]
    },
