      "explanation": "Hans Scharoun (1893–1972 - Bremen & Berlin) and the organic revolution of concert hall architecture:\n- **Hans Scharoun**:\n  - The visionary master of German Organic Architecture (*Organische Architektur*); member of the Expressionist *Glass Chain* (*Gläserne Kette*);\n- The Monumental Masterpiece: **Berliner Philharmonie (Kulturforum, Berlin - Inaugurated October 15, 1963 under Herbert von Karajan)**:\n  - The **most influential concert hall design in modern acoustic history**;\n  - **The \"Vineyard\" Seating Principle (Das Weinberg-Prinzip)**:\n    - Abolished the traditional hierarchical rectangular \"shoebox\" hall (where the orchestra sits at the front and audience sits passively behind);\n    - Placed the **orchestra directly at the center of the hall**, with 2,440 audience seats arranged around the stage in terraced, sloping asymmetrical \"vineyard\" blocks, creating an intimate, egalitarian community experience where no listener is far from the musicians;\n    - Acoustic Engineering: Designed with acoustician Lothar Cremer; three-tiered convex ceiling resembling a dynamic tent, with floating acoustic \"clouds\" (*Schallreflektoren*);\n    - Copied worldwide (Sydney Opera House, Walt Disney Concert Hall in LA, Elbphilharmonie Hamburg, Philharmonie de Paris);\n  - **Staatsbibliothek zu Berlin (Potsdamer Straße - 1978)**: Monumental terraced reading landscape.",
